{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC1 — the 449f hypothesis is **REFUTED**, decisively, with runtime evidence from the very run it was raised against.\n\n## Experiment 1 — the mechanism itself (local, git 2.55.0)\n\nBuilt a bare origin with `main` + `tickets`, then a workspace whose `remote.origin.fetch`\nwas set explicitly, emulating reconcile-bridge.yml:115 (explicit refspec creates\n`origin/tickets`), then let a third party advance the true remote branch, then ran the\nsite under test — a bare `git fetch origin tickets`:\n\n    configured +refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main   (narrow)\n      origin/tickets before : 3c477492\n      true remote tickets   : 2322aded\n      FETCH_HEAD after      : 2322aded\n      origin/tickets after  : 3c477492   -> STALE\n\n    configured +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*         (wildcard)\n      origin/tickets before : 3c477492\n      true remote tickets   : 2322aded\n      origin/tickets after  : 2322aded   -> ADVANCED\n\nSo the stale-ref mechanism is real — but it is conditional on a NARROW configured refspec.\n\n## Experiment 2 — is that precondition true in CI? NO.\n\n`actions/checkout` builds its workspace with `git remote add origin <url>`, and\n`git remote add` installs the DEFAULT WILDCARD refspec (verified directly:\n`git init && git remote add origin <url>` -> `remote.origin.fetch = +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*`).\nThe real runner log for run **30585738860** — one of the four cited mode-2 push-rejection\nfailures — confirms it: `git remote add origin https://github.com/navapbc/rebar` followed by\n`git -c protocol.version=2 fetch ... origin +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/* +refs/tags/*:refs/tags/*`,\nwith no `git config remote.origin.fetch` narrowing anywhere in the job.\n\n## Experiment 3 — direct observation of the ref in the failing run\n\nThe same run log shows the bare fetch UPDATING the remote-tracking ref on every single\nretry:\n\n    60ccc6506..4d37b6925  tickets -> origin/tickets\n    4d37b6925..dd70be0d7  tickets -> origin/tickets\n    dd70be0d7..a46013f4c  tickets -> origin/tickets\n    a46013f4c..f3a1fe61f  tickets -> origin/tickets\n\nFour distinct advances, each followed by a \"Merge made by the 'ort' strategy\" integrating\nnew event files. That is the exact opposite of the predicted stale-ref signature, which\nrequires the ref to stay pinned. 449f mode 2 was a genuine push race — as 449f already\nconcluded and fixed under 4c4f (backoff removal) — not a stale merge target.\n\n**Conclusion: the workflow bare-fetch sites were NOT causing 449f, and are not causing any\nobserved failure.** No link to 449f is being made; the hypothesis is dead. The workflow\nchange in this ticket is therefore hardening/consistency, not a defect fix: it removes a\ndependency on a third-party action's internal choice of `git remote add`, and it is a\nbehavioural no-op today.\n\n## What IS a live defect: push.py:182\n\nDrove the real `push.push_tickets_branch` under a narrow refspec with a competing writer\non origin/tickets. Only the configured refspec differs between the two runs:\n\n    narrow   -> \"tickets branch push failed (merge conflict, attempt 1/2/3)\"\n                \"tickets branch push failed after 3 retries\"\n                PUSH CONVERGED: False | competing events absorbed: False\n    wildcard -> PUSH CONVERGED: True  | competing events absorbed: True\n\nThe bare fetch leaves `origin/tickets` absent, `git merge origin/tickets` fails, the loop\nmisreports it as a merge conflict, and every retry re-pushes the same rejected history.\nThis reaches ordinary users: a single-branch/`--depth` clone is exactly the configuration\nbug 5546 proved occurs for this repo/branch/remote triple.\n\n## Scope note\n\n`push.py`'s merge carries NO `--allow-unrelated-histories` (verified), so reaching the\nmerge cannot union unrelated histories. 5546's `_carries_ticket_events` merge-safety\npredicate has no analogue here and was deliberately NOT replicated — this change is a\nrefspec change and nothing else."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785532337989561001,"uuid":"d1e6e6eb-4c72-4ce3-a1ea-46a539256961"}